SUMMARY:Experimental/Behavioural – James Bland (UToledo) – Meta-study i
 n the shadow of the future: the effect of continuation probability on stra
 tegies
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3053
DESCRIPTION:James Bland: “Meta-study in the shadow of the future: the eff
 ect of continuation probability on strategies”\nhttps://sites.google.com
 /site/jamesbland/\n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/82654401351?pwd=V6TMN
 2ZpNRILVKO2Y5za5S7GjfbAGG.1&from=addon\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.app.go
 o.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nThis paper develops methodology for e
 stimating and aggregating causal effects for latent outcome variables. Usi
 ng data from forty treatments in eleven economic experiments\, I estimate 
 and aggregate causal effects of the continuation probability on players’
  strategy choice and first-round cooperation in the indefinitely repeated 
 Prisoner’s Dilemma. Increasing the continuation probability significantl
 y and substantially increases the probability that a participant will coop
 erate in the first round of a supergame\, and use the “Grim trigger”\,
  “Tit-for-tat”\, and “Tit-for-two-tats” strategies. Increasing the
  continuation probability decreases the probability that a participant wil
 l use the “Always defect” and “Suspicious tit-for-tat” strategies.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/82654401351?pwd=V6TMN2ZpNRILVKO2Y5za5S7GjfbAG
 G.1&from=addon
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:b43c635a340d442cc9e967b5cd34fe0a@generated
DTSTAMP:20260808T192131Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260808T192131Z
SEQUENCE:0
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20260813T111500
DTEND;TZID=Australia/Melbourne:20260813T123000
SUMMARY:Experimental/Behavioural – Kirby Nielsen (Caltech) – Rules in t
 he Face of Complexity
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3053
DESCRIPTION:Kirby Nielsen: “Rules in the Face of Complexity”\nhttps://k
 irbyknielsen.com/\n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/82654401351?pwd=V6TMN
 2ZpNRILVKO2Y5za5S7GjfbAGG.1&from=addon\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.app.go
 o.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nWe show that complexity can affect ho
 w people approach decision environments (the ''extensive margin'')\, and a
 lso can affect choices conditional on a given approach (the ''intensive ma
 rgin''). On the extensive margin\, we hypothesize that individuals resort 
 to ''rules''---simplified choice processes that we measure as being easier
  to describe to another person---as the complexity of the decision environ
 ment increases. We test our hypothesis using an experiment by exogenously 
 varying the complexity of the decision environment and measuring how well 
 another individual can replicate the decision-maker's choices given the de
 cision-maker's description of how they chose. We find that individuals' ch
 oice processes are more describable in complex choice environments\, which
  we interpret as evidence that decision-making becomes more rule-like as c
 omplexity increases. Additional secondary evidence suggests that rule-base
 d decision-making is a choice simplification that reduces the cognitive co
 sts of decision-making. On the intensive margin\, when we exogenously fix 
 the choice process\, we find that complexity leads to more decision noise.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/82654401351?pwd=V6TMN2ZpNRILVKO2Y5za5S7GjfbAG
 G.1&from=addon
END:VEVENT
